  "notes": "Drawn from LV-CXT (left, 2017 and 2024) and LV-GUC (right, 2020); the 737 MAXs (LV-GVA) wear the same. No dated photo from below was found: the belly aft of the nose is taken as the white base. Fine gold-and-white 'sun ray' lines cross the fin's celeste near the trailing edge and the condor sweep; too fine to draw. Small marks vary by airframe and aren't drawn: an 'Argentina' country-brand logo (LV-CXT) or a round sun emblem (LV-GUC) by the aft door, the type ('BOEING 737-800') over the aft windows. The A330s and the ex-Austral E190s wear the same scheme. The silver pinstripe under the blue is seen clearly only on the nose. Re-checked 2026-11-15 on LV-GUC (737-800, the commonest type), placed by its doors and overwing exits: the wordmark runs 0.142 to 0.392 (length 0.25), its lowercase about 0.58 of the capitals (a low-resolution measure); the celeste's lower edge and the pinstripe are curves, now drawn smooth (the pinstripe's square aft end kept as corners).",
  "history": "Replaces Skyarium v1's approximation. The 2010 scheme replaced the white scheme with dark blue and aqua cheatlines; no newer standard scheme has been announced as of 2026."
